              "type": "summary_text",
              "text": "**Setting Up TypeScript Paths**\n\nFor configuring paths, I suggest setting it as \"paths\": { \"bun\": [\"./src/compat/module.ts\"] } only, and not using \"bun/*\" unless there are submodules. Since their code uses just \"bun\", that's sufficient. If they plan to use something like \"bun:test\", they’ll need separate mappings like \"bun:test\": [\"./src/compat/test.ts\"]. TypeScript can't map colon patterns with wildcards, so each has to be specified individually. Also, Node resolution won’t honor TypeScript paths directly; they’ll need a bundler or loader, or possibly tsx, which supports path aliases through tsconfig."
            },
            {
              "type": "summary_text",
              "text": "**Checking TSX and Module Resolution**\n\nI think I should clarify that TSX, which is esbuild-based, resolves tsconfig paths by default. TSX v4 utilizes \"jiti,\" but there’s another tool focusing on \"tsconfig paths\" support. The TSX documentation states it automatically supports tsconfig.json paths and baseUrl. So, mapping \"bun\" should make it resolve correctly at runtime, but Node itself won’t handle that. Instead, running tsx will compile with esbuild, which does support these paths. Node 22 brings customizable loaders and experimental import maps, but it's best to rely on a bundler or loader instead. Also, I need to confirm if `ModuleResolution` is related to the new TS 5.0."
            },
            {
              "type": "summary_text",
              "text": "**Clarifying TS and Node Setup**\n\nFor using \"bun\", I recommend mapping it simply as \"bun\". Avoid adding \"bun/*\" unless creating subpath modules, as it won't match others like \"bun:test\". If using paths for tests, separate alias mappings may be needed. \n\nRegarding \"moduleResolution: Bundler\": it's compatible with tsx and Node 22, favoring ESM. Ensure \"module\": \"esnext\" and consider enabling \"allowSyntheticDefaultImports\" and \"esModuleInterop\". Remember, Node might not directly resolve \"bun\", so using export maps in package.json may be necessary. Lastly, use bundlers like esbuild or vite for production to handle paths accurately."
            },
            {
              "type": "summary_text",
              "text": "**Configuring TypeScript and JSX Settings**\n\nIt's important to ensure that your build configuration matches the aliasing; tsx resolves tsconfig paths automatically, while esbuild and vite require the tsconfigPaths plugin. Tsup, which uses esbuild, supports tsconfig paths by default—making it safer to configure.\n\nFor JSX settings with Solid, keep \"jsx\": \"preserve\" and \"jsxImportSource\": \"solid-js\". If you have external .tsx files, it's essential to maintain these settings. \n\nHere's a concise example of the tsconfig:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"compilerOptions\": {\n    \"target\": \"ESNext\",\n    \"lib\": [\"ESNext\", \"DOM\"],\n    \"module\": \"ESNext\",\n    \"moduleResolution\": \"Bundler\",\n    \"verbatimModuleSyntax\": true,\n    \"types\": [\"node\"],\n    \"jsx\": \"preserve\",\n    \"jsxImportSource\": \"solid-js\",\n    \"baseUrl\": \".\",\n    \"paths\": {\n      \"@/*\": [\"./src/*\"],\n      \"bun\": [\"./src/compat/module.ts\"]\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\nAdditionally, adding \"skipLibCheck\": true is recommended, and if you run into compatibility issues, consider using \"NodeNext\". It’s also best to include \"type\": \"module\" in package.json for ESM support."
