              "text": "Short answers\n1) Use \"bun\" (exact) for your codebase as shown. Add explicit entries like \"bun:test\" only if you actually import those. \"bun/*\" won’t match specifiers like \"bun:test\" (colon), and you shouldn’t add it unless you truly import from \"bun/...\" (slash).\n2) moduleResolution: \"Bundler\" works well with Node 22 + tsx. Biggest gotcha is that it assumes modern “exports”-based packages and ESM. Some older CJS-only packages may not type-resolve cleanly; if that bites you, either:\n   - keep \"Bundler\" and add esModuleInterop, allowSyntheticDefaultImports, skipLibCheck; or\n   - switch to \"NodeNext\".\n   Also remember TS paths only affect the type-check/build step; make sure your runtime/build tool (tsx/esbuild/tsup/vite) honors tsconfig paths.\n3) Yes, keep JSX settings for Solid. Typically \"jsx\": \"preserve\" and \"jsxImportSource\": \"solid-js\". Keep them wherever you have .tsx using Solid (even if it’s TUI and not the DOM).\n\nDetails and recommendations\n\nPath alias for “bun”\n- Your imports are from \"bun\" exactly (both values and types), so this is sufficient:\n  \"paths\": { \"bun\": [\"./src/compat/module.ts\"] }\n- If you also import “bun:test”, “bun:sqlite”, etc., add explicit mappings:\n  \"paths\": {\n    \"bun\": [\"./src/compat/module.ts\"],\n    \"bun:test\": [\"./src/compat/test.ts\"]\n  }\n- \"bun/*\" only helps for specifiers like \"bun/foo\" (with a slash). It does not match \"bun:test\".\n\nBundler resolution with tsx (Node 22+)\n- \"Bundler\" is a good fit with tsx and modern ESM:\n  - Pros: aligns with exports/imports, good for libraries/bundlers, works with path aliases.\n  - Typical flags to pair with it:\n    - \"verbatimModuleSyntax\": true (recommended with bundler-style setups)\n    - \"esModuleInterop\": true and/or \"allowSyntheticDefaultImports\": true (to smooth interop with CJS)\n    - \"skipLibCheck\": true (pragmatic when third-party types get noisy)\n- If you hit resolution/type issues with older packages (no exports field, only main):\n  - Try switching to \"moduleResolution\": \"NodeNext\".\n- Runtime note: Node itself won’t know your TS paths. tsx does, and most bundlers can be configured to. Ensure your build/dev tools honor tsconfig paths:\n  - tsx: supports tsconfig paths\n  - esbuild/tsup: often do; verify or add a tsconfig-paths plugin if needed\n  - vite: add vite-tsconfig-paths plugin\n\nJSX/Solid\n- Keep:\n  - \"jsx\": \"preserve\"\n  - \"jsxImportSource\": \"solid-js\"\n- If you’re using a transform that expects the automatic runtime, you can also use \"jsx\": \"react-jsx\" with the same jsxImportSource, but “preserve” is the safer default with build tools doing the JSX transform.\n- You can add \"lib\": [\"ESNext\", \"DOM\"] if you rely on DOM typings for Solid’s JSX types (safe even in Node-only runtime).\n\nSuggested tsconfig snippet (root and package, adjust paths as needed)\n{\n  \"compilerOptions\": {\n    \"target\": \"ESNext\",\n    \"module\": \"ESNext\",\n    \"moduleResolution\": \"Bundler\",\n    \"verbatimModuleSyntax\": true,\n    \"esModuleInterop\": true,\n    \"allowSyntheticDefaultImports\": true,\n    \"skipLibCheck\": true,\n\n    \"types\": [\"node\"],\n    \"jsx\": \"preserve\",\n    \"jsxImportSource\": \"solid-js\",\n\n    \"baseUrl\": \".\",\n    \"paths\": {\n      \"@/*\": [\"./src/*\"],\n      \"@tui/*\": [\"./src/cli/cmd/tui/*\"],\n      \"bun\": [\"./src/compat/module.ts\"]\n      // Add only if used:\n      // \"bun:test\": [\"./src/compat/test.ts\"]\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nOther small checks\n- Ensure package.json has \"type\": \"module\" if you’re writing ESM (recommended with the above).\n- Top-level await is fine in Node 22 ESM with module: ESNext.\n- If you have both a root tsconfig and per-package tsconfig, keep them consistent (have packages extend the root base and override only what differs)."
